WEBVTT

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.882
Welcome to the last video.

00:00:05.882 --> 00:00:08.925
It covers communications and the tools you'll use.

00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:09.899
First, HF radio.

00:00:09.899 --> 00:00:15.011
VHF is clear, but it works only in line of sight, about two hundred and fifty miles.

00:00:15.011 --> 00:00:17.347
Over the ocean, aircraft use HF radio,

00:00:17.347 --> 00:00:21.158
which bounces off the ionosphere and carries over the horizon.

00:00:21.158 --> 00:00:22.376
The price is static.

00:00:22.376 --> 00:00:24.775
On VATSIM, many aircraft can't tune HF,

00:00:24.775 --> 00:00:27.975
so every oceanic position has a VHF alias frequency.

00:00:27.975 --> 00:00:32.296
Pilots tune that, and the audio software treats it as the HF frequency.

00:00:32.296 --> 00:00:34.609
You'll see both in the position table.

00:00:35.909 --> 00:00:40.053
Even pilots who plan to use CPDLC must first check in with you by voice.

00:00:40.053 --> 00:00:42.010
That call includes a SELCAL check.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:46.442
SELCAL tells a pilot you want to speak to them, so they can turn the HF down.

00:00:46.442 --> 00:00:51.565
In the real world, you trigger four tones, and the aircraft with that code gets an alert.

00:00:51.565 --> 00:00:54.009
On VATSIM, the pilot client does that job,

00:00:54.009 --> 00:00:56.860
using the code the pilot entered when logging in.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.256
Check SELCAL on first contact with each oceanic controller.

00:01:00.256 --> 00:01:05.379
In EuroScope, type dot selcal followed by the code, or dot selcal and click the aircraft.

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:08.372
In vNAAATS, use the SELCAL tab in the aircraft menu.

00:01:09.672 --> 00:01:13.236
Now phraseology, using the Gander Oceanic phraseology sheet.

00:01:13.236 --> 00:01:17.216
Oceanic phraseology differs from domestic in small, important ways.

00:01:17.216 --> 00:01:19.135
Say numbers one digit at a time,

00:01:19.135 --> 00:01:23.573
and letters from the phonetic alphabet, like Speedbird one-two-four Romeo.

00:01:23.573 --> 00:01:25.652
Say Mach as Mach decimal eight-six.

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:28.978
Say the NAT track with its phonetic letter, track alpha.

00:01:28.978 --> 00:01:31.915
Say latitudes as two digits, like five-one north,

00:01:31.915 --> 00:01:35.810
and longitudes as two or three digits, like zero-three-zero west.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:39.255
Never use the words super or heavy in Gander and Shanwick.

00:01:40.555 --> 00:01:43.373
When you hand an aircraft over, the word matters.

00:01:43.373 --> 00:01:45.348
For another oceanic radio station,

00:01:45.348 --> 00:01:49.124
say call, as in call Shanwick Radio on one-three-one-point-eight.

00:01:49.124 --> 00:01:54.070
For a radar controller, say contact, as in contact Moncton on one-three-two-point-two.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.681
And for UNICOM, say monitor.

00:01:56.981 --> 00:01:59.994
Here is the clearance request, from the Gander sheet.

00:01:59.994 --> 00:02:03.861
The pilot calls: Gander Radio, ACA489, requesting oceanic clearance.

00:02:03.861 --> 00:02:05.567
You answer: pass your message.

00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:07.404
The pilot gives the destination,

00:02:07.404 --> 00:02:11.537
the track, the entry fix and estimate, and the level and Mach they want.

00:02:11.537 --> 00:02:16.029
You say: Gander Radio, oceanic clearance, and the pilot replies: ready to copy.

00:02:16.029 --> 00:02:18.588
A short delay to check for conflicts is fine.

00:02:18.588 --> 00:02:21.056
If the clearance isn't what they asked for,

00:02:21.056 --> 00:02:24.729
say so first, for example oceanic clearance with a level change.

00:02:26.029 --> 00:02:29.515
Then you issue it: Gander Radio clears ACA489 to Boston via DOGAL,

00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:34.057
track alpha, from DOGAL maintain flight level three-nine-zero, Mach decimal eight-six.

00:02:34.057 --> 00:02:37.363
The pilot reads it back, and the readback must include the TMI.

00:02:37.363 --> 00:02:38.727
If it doesn't, ask for it.

00:02:38.727 --> 00:02:40.406
You don't give the TMI yourself.

00:02:40.406 --> 00:02:42.630
When it's correct, say: read back correct,

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:46.125
return to previous frequency and call Gander Radio overhead DOGAL.

00:02:47.425 --> 00:02:52.199
Random routes don't need a TMI, but you read out every waypoint in latitude and longitude,

00:02:52.199 --> 00:02:54.002
and the pilot reads them all back.

00:02:54.002 --> 00:02:57.086
For a Tango route, you clear the aircraft via Tango Route,

00:02:57.086 --> 00:02:59.053
routing, for example, LASNO to BEGAS.

00:02:59.053 --> 00:03:02.659
Time restrictions are added only when they're needed for separation,

00:03:02.659 --> 00:03:05.735
as in: cross MALOT not before time one-zero-zero-two Zulu.

00:03:05.735 --> 00:03:06.945
They must be read back.

00:03:08.245 --> 00:03:10.235
Now the first call on your frequency.

00:03:10.235 --> 00:03:12.728
An ADS-B equipped aircraft says: Gander Radio,

00:03:12.728 --> 00:03:16.902
ACA489, overhead MALOT, flight level three-five-zero, Mach decimal eight-two.

00:03:16.902 --> 00:03:19.699
Check that the level and Mach match their clearance.

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:21.384
Then reply with their position,

00:03:21.384 --> 00:03:24.645
level and Mach, and tell them to standby for a SELCAL check.

00:03:24.645 --> 00:03:29.216
After the check, say: roger, remain on SELCAL watch and no position reports required.

00:03:30.516 --> 00:03:33.721
Aircraft that aren't ADS-B equipped check in the same way,

00:03:33.721 --> 00:03:36.097
but you ask them to pass a position report.

00:03:36.097 --> 00:03:37.590
They give the fix and time,

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:41.240
the level, the next fix with its estimate, and the fix after that.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.156
You read it back, and they confirm.

00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:45.638
Then they repeat the report at each waypoint,

00:03:45.638 --> 00:03:49.831
and you give the next sector's details after their last report in your area.

00:03:49.831 --> 00:03:52.375
On VATSIM, voice position reports are optional

00:03:52.375 --> 00:03:54.920
for ADS-B aircraft and mandatory for the rest.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:58.969
A pilot can also call with a revised estimate, and you read that back too.

00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:05.273
For a level change, an ADS-B aircraft that asks to climb to flight level three-seven-zero

00:04:05.273 --> 00:04:09.941
hears: Gander Radio clears you to climb and maintain flight level three-seven-zero.

00:04:09.941 --> 00:04:12.233
For other aircraft, add: report reaching.

00:04:12.233 --> 00:04:15.958
And at the boundary, use the right word: call for a radio station,

00:04:15.958 --> 00:04:18.103
contact for radar, monitor for UNICOM.

00:04:19.403 --> 00:04:20.153
Next, CPDLC.

00:04:20.153 --> 00:04:23.214
It's text messaging between pilot and controller.

00:04:23.214 --> 00:04:26.462
On the network it runs over the Hoppie ACARS system.

00:04:26.462 --> 00:04:30.710
You need your own Hoppie logon code, which you get at hoppie dot nl.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:38.889
In vNAAATS, open the CPDLC drawer, enter your code, and the station is chosen from your position: CZQX for Gander,

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:41.543
EGGX for Shanwick, and NATX for the bandbox.

00:04:41.543 --> 00:04:44.002
Only one controller can use each station.

00:04:44.002 --> 00:04:46.239
If you use the Topsky plugin instead,

00:04:46.239 --> 00:04:51.317
turn Auto-accept logon off, so no aircraft logs on before you've made voice contact.

00:04:52.617 --> 00:04:57.284
The rule is simple: voice contact and a SELCAL check first, then accept the logon.

00:04:57.284 --> 00:05:00.187
A new logon plays a sound and flashes the callsign.

00:05:00.187 --> 00:05:04.001
A logged-on aircraft has solid square brackets around its callsign.

00:05:04.001 --> 00:05:08.270
From then on, the level and Mach you change are sent to the pilot by CPDLC.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:11.685
Brackets show on the new data until the pilot answers WILCO.

00:05:11.685 --> 00:05:13.905
If they say UNABLE, the change reverts.

00:05:13.905 --> 00:05:16.837
If you've already agreed a change on the frequency,

00:05:16.837 --> 00:05:19.768
choose the voice option so you don't send it twice.

00:05:21.068 --> 00:05:24.865
You can reply with quick buttons, such as unable, standby and roger.

00:05:24.865 --> 00:05:29.499
To hand off, choose the next controller, and the pilot is sent the contact message.

00:05:29.499 --> 00:05:31.751
When no further controller is available,

00:05:31.751 --> 00:05:36.535
use the UNICOM message, and let the pilot answer before you end the CPDLC connection.

00:05:37.835 --> 00:05:40.403
Now vNAAATS, the toolset you'll use to control.

00:05:40.403 --> 00:05:44.699
It has three parts that share one set of flight data: the Flight Data Display,

00:05:44.699 --> 00:05:47.068
a EuroScope plugin, and the vNAAATS server.

00:05:47.068 --> 00:05:50.051
Because of that, every controller sees the same level,

00:05:50.051 --> 00:05:52.149
Mach, track and SELCAL within seconds.

00:05:53.449 --> 00:05:56.282
The Flight Data Display, the FDD, is where you work.

00:05:56.282 --> 00:05:59.713
Every oceanic flight is a strip, grouped by track or entry fix.

00:05:59.713 --> 00:06:04.615
You can change flight data, track and transfer aircraft, send SELCAL checks and run CPDLC.

00:06:04.615 --> 00:06:06.739
Click a callsign for the aircraft menu.

00:06:06.739 --> 00:06:09.190
The display doesn't check separation for you.

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:10.388
That's still your job.

00:06:11.688 --> 00:06:15.943
To get started, get your personal key at vnaaats dot com slash get-key.

00:06:15.943 --> 00:06:20.558
Gander controllers can also take it from their profile on the Gander website.

00:06:20.558 --> 00:06:21.817
It lasts ninety days.

00:06:21.817 --> 00:06:24.841
Install FDD for Windows, which sets up the plugin,

00:06:24.841 --> 00:06:28.410
the airline list and the font, and opens EuroScope for you.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:32.006
Follow the setup screen, and it takes about fifteen minutes.

00:06:32.006 --> 00:06:37.204
Then each day, open the app, connect to VATSIM as an oceanic position such as NAT_FSS,

00:06:37.204 --> 00:06:39.138
and the display opens by itself.

00:06:40.438 --> 00:06:44.771
The natTRAK boxes show pending and cleared requests, with a sound for new requests.

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:49.504
The display can also send the pilot an automatic reminder to request clearance on natTRAK,

00:06:49.504 --> 00:06:51.976
at least thirty minutes before the entry point.

00:06:51.976 --> 00:06:55.709
If something goes wrong, check the problems page in the Knowledge Base,

00:06:55.709 --> 00:06:59.233
and the full manual and videos are at vnaaats dot com slash manual.

00:07:00.533 --> 00:07:01.499
That's everything.

00:07:01.499 --> 00:07:04.155
When you pass the exam, submit your availability,

00:07:04.155 --> 00:07:07.461
and an instructor will book your live session on the network.

00:07:07.461 --> 00:07:12.056
Under a shared agreement with VATSIM UK, you're trained for both Gander and Shanwick,

00:07:12.056 --> 00:07:15.463
so your live session is taken on NAT_FSS and covers both sides.

00:07:15.463 --> 00:07:17.635
Bring what you've learned: the airspace,

00:07:17.635 --> 00:07:20.404
the routes, separation, phraseology and your tools.

00:07:20.404 --> 00:07:23.059
The instructor will guide you, and when you pass,

00:07:23.059 --> 00:07:26.311
you're given your Oceanic endorsement and your Discord role.

00:07:26.311 --> 00:07:27.385
Welcome to the team.
